This version tries the two choices for every element: include it or leave it out. Results are saved by `(index, remaining_sum)`, which prevents the same smaller problem from being solved again.

```python
class Solution:
    def isSubsetSum(self, arr, target):
        memo = {}

        def can_make(index, remaining):
            if remaining == 0:
                return True
            if index == len(arr) or remaining < 0:
                return False

            state = (index, remaining)
            if state in memo:
                return memo[state]

            skip = can_make(index + 1, remaining)
            take = can_make(index + 1, remaining - arr[index])
            memo[state] = skip or take
            return memo[state]

        return can_make(0, target)
```

For `[3, 4, 6]` and target `10`, the call that takes `4` continues with remaining sum `6`, which can then be completed by the last value.

The base case for target zero is important because an empty chosen subset is a valid way to make zero. Recursion also adds call-stack space, and this style is not ideal when the target or array is very large.

Time complexity: `O(n * target)` in the usual non-negative input range  
Space complexity: `O(n * target)` for the memo table, plus recursion depth

A set gives a direct way to keep all sums that can currently be formed. For each number, a second set is built from the old sums, then both sets are combined. Using a separate set is important because it keeps one array value from being picked twice.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSubsetSum(int[] arr, int target) {
        Set<Integer> reachable = new HashSet<>();
        reachable.add(0);

        for (int value : arr) {
            Set<Integer> added = new HashSet<>();

            for (int sum : reachable) {
                int next = sum + value;
                if (next == target) {
                    return true;
                }
                if (next < target) {
                    added.add(next);
                }
            }

            reachable.addAll(added);
        }

        return target == 0;
    }
}
```

For `arr = [2, 5, 8]` and target `7`, the reachable sums start as `{0}`, then become `{0, 2}`, and finally include `7` after adding `5`.

This handles target zero immediately through the initial sum of zero. The set can be smaller than a full target-sized table when few sums are possible, but hash-set operations have more overhead.

Time complexity: `O(n * r)` on average, where `r` is the number of stored sums  
Space complexity: `O(r)`

A two-dimensional table makes the choices easy to follow. `dp[i][s]` is true when sum `s` can be made using the first `i` values. For each value, the code either skips it or includes it.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSubsetSum(int[] arr, int target) {
        int n = arr.length;
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n + 1][target + 1];
        dp[0][0] = true;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            int value = arr[i - 1];
            for (int sum = 0; sum <= target; sum++) {
                dp[i][sum] = dp[i - 1][sum];

                if (sum >= value) {
                    dp[i][sum] = dp[i][sum] || dp[i - 1][sum - value];
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[n][target];
    }
}
```

For example, with `arr = [2, 3, 7]` and `target = 5`, the entry for sum `5` becomes true after using `2` and `3`.

This stores a full table, so it uses more memory than needed, but it is simple to inspect. An empty array returns true only when the target is zero.

Time complexity: `O(n * target)`  
Space complexity: `O(n * target)`

The array is scanned once while a boolean DP array stores the sums already possible. The loop over sums goes backward so one number cannot be used more than once in the same step.

```cpp
class Solution{   
public:
    bool isSubsetSum(vector<int>arr, int target){
        vector<bool> possible(target + 1, false);
        possible[0] = true;

        for (int value : arr) {
            for (int sum = target; sum >= value; sum--) {
                possible[sum] = possible[sum] || possible[sum - value];
            }
        }

        return possible[target];
    }
};
```

A quick dry run for `[1, 4]` and target `5`: after `1`, sums `0` and `1` are possible. After `4`, sum `5` is marked from the earlier sum `1`.

The input is passed by value in the given template, so the original array is not changed. This method expects non-negative values, which is the usual version of this problem.

Time complexity: `O(n * target)`  
Space complexity: `O(target)`

class Solution{   
public:

    bool solve(vector<int>& arr, int target, int i,  vector<vector<int>>& dp ){

        if(target == 0){
            return true;
        }

        if(i >= arr.size()){
            return false;
        }

        if(dp[i][target] != -1){
            return dp[i][target];
        }



        bool take = false;
        bool not_take = false;

        if(arr[i] <= target){
            take = solve(arr, target - arr[i], i+1, dp);
        }

        not_take = solve(arr, target, i + 1, dp);

        return dp[i][target] = take || not_take;
    }
    bool isSubsetSum(vector<int>arr, int target){
        int n = arr.size();
        vector<vector<int>> dp (n, vector<int>(target + 1, -1));
        return solve(arr, target, 0, dp);
    }
};
```

